  },
  "articleBody": "When you've got damaged trim, one of the first questions is always whether to repair a small section or replace the entire piece. It really depends on the extent and type of damage. If it's just a small crack, a minor ding, or superficial rot that hasn't spread deep into the wood, a localized repair can often do the trick. We'll carefully remove the compromised material, patch it with a suitable filler, then sand, seal, and paint to blend it seamlessly. However, if the damage is widespread, if the trim is heavily rotted, or if there's significant water damage that has spread beyond a small area, then full replacement is usually the better, more durable option. This is especially true if the trim's structural integrity is compromised. We always check for hidden moisture damage too, because just patching the visible part without addressing underlying issues is a common mistake that leads to recurring problems. We'll walk you through what we find and explain whether a repair or replacement makes the most sense for your home, ensuring the fix lasts.",
